   311	#### Paste-ready FAQ replacement
   312	
   313	Replace the existing FAQ heading and answers with:
   314	
   315	> ### FAQs
   316	>
   317	> **1. How much does bariatric surgery cost in Perth?**
   318	>
   319	> There is no single Perth price. Total costs vary by procedure, hospital, surgeon, anaesthetist, tests, private-health cover, excess and waiting periods. Any range or estimate should be discussed at consultation with an itemised breakdown; ask your insurer which benefits and gaps apply before booking.
   320	>
   321	> **2. Is bariatric surgery covered by Medicare or private health insurance?**
   322	>
   323	> Medicare benefits may apply when the relevant MBS item, clinical and referral requirements are met, but eligibility is assessed individually. Verify the planned item number: 31575 is sleeve gastrectomy, 31572 is Roux-en-Y gastric bypass, and 31581 is BPD/duodenal switch. Private-health benefits depend on the fund, policy, waiting periods and exclusions.
   324	>
   325	> **3. How do I know which bariatric procedure is right for me?**
   326	>
   327	> The choice depends on your medical history, eating patterns, health conditions, goals and willingness to follow the required aftercare. Your surgeon should explain likely benefits, limits, risks and monitoring needs for each option; no procedure or outcome is guaranteed.
   328	>
   329	> **4. What is the difference between a gastric sleeve and a gastric bypass?**
   330	>
   331	> A sleeve removes part of the stomach without rerouting the small intestine. A Roux-en-Y bypass creates a smaller stomach pouch and reroutes part of the small intestine. Each has different risks, nutritional monitoring and follow-up needs, which your surgeon can explain for your circumstances.
   332	>
   333	> **5. Do I have to have surgery, or are there non-surgical options?**
   334	>
   335	> Surgery is not the only pathway. Some people may discuss medical weight management, dietitian-led support or a staged approach before considering surgery. The clinical team should assess which options are appropriate and explain their limits, follow-up requirements and risks.
   336	>
   337	> **6. What is the 30/30 eating rule after bariatric surgery?**
   338	>
   339	> Many bariatric programs use a 30/30 approach: take about 30 minutes to eat and separate drinks from meals by about 30 minutes. It is a general postoperative guide, not a universal rule; your surgeon or dietitian will tailor advice to your procedure and stage of recovery.
   340	>
   341	> **7. Who may not be eligible for bariatric surgery?**
   342	>
   343	> No online checklist can decide suitability. For MBS items, a BMI below 35 does not meet the threshold; a procedure may also be deferred if assessment identifies unacceptable surgical or anaesthetic risk, unresolved medical issues or difficulty completing required follow-up. Your surgeon makes an individual assessment.
   344	>
   345	> **8. What does aftercare involve, and what can happen over 10 years?**
   346	>
   347	> Aftercare can include surgeon reviews, blood tests, dietitian support, supplements where prescribed and help adapting eating. Over 10 years, experiences vary: some people maintain changes, some regain weight, develop nutritional issues or need further treatment. No single 10-year result can be promised, so ongoing follow-up remains important.
   348	>
   349	> **9. Where will my surgery and appointments take place?**
   350	>
   351	> Appointments take place at the confirmed OSWA clinic locations, and surgery takes place at the hospital selected with your surgeon. Hospital names and affiliations must be verified before publication; the team should confirm the location, admission process and follow-up arrangements during planning.
   352	>
   353	> **10. I have had weight loss surgery before — can it be revised?**
   354	>
   355	> Revision surgery may be discussed after a previous procedure when there are complications, a need for conversion or another clinical reason. It can involve different risks and follow-up from a first procedure, so the surgeon needs to review your operative history and current health before advising.
   356	>
   357	> **11. How should I choose a bariatric surgeon in Perth?**
   358	>
   359	> Ask about specialist registration or FRACS status, relevant bariatric experience, verified hospital access, multidisciplinary aftercare and how risks, costs and complications are explained. Ask who provides follow-up and what happens if concerns arise. Avoid claims that any individual surgeon or clinic is “the best.”
