+- **Merge the risk bullets** (source lines 218-246):
+
+  > Every surgical procedure carries risk, though modern keyhole techniques and highly experienced surgeons make bariatric procedures safe for most suitable candidates. Below are some of the most common risks, potential complications, and side effects of undergoing bariatric surgery:
+  >
+  > General surgical risks include bleeding, infection, blood clots, and adverse reactions to anaesthetic — these apply to any surgical procedure.
+  >
+  > Procedure-specific risks include leaks at staple or join lines (sleeve and bypass) and band slippage or erosion (gastric band).
+  >
+  > Nutritional changes are common, particularly with bypass procedures, which affect vitamin and mineral absorption and require lifelong supplementation and monitoring.
+  >
+  > Eating habits will change — smaller portions, slower eating, and in some cases avoiding certain foods, particularly in the early months following surgery.
+  >
+  > Weight regain is possible if lifestyle habits aren't sustained; surgery is a powerful tool but works best alongside ongoing dietary and behavioural support.
